  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 313 AM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nA drastic shift in the large-scale pattern will initiate mid-week as \nsouthern and northern-stream shortwaves slide into the Southeast US \nand start to dig out a longwave trough over the Eastern US that will \npersist into the weekend. \n\nOn Wednesday, conditions will remain benign across North and Central \nGA with mild temperatures and increasing cloud cover and moisture \nassociated with the southern-stream shortwave sliding over the Gulf \nCoast states. Outside of some very isolated spots of light rain, \nmost of the area will be dry. As moisture and forcing continue to \nincrease Wednesday night and the stronger northern shortwave moves \ninto the area, scattered rain showers will develop, primarily across \nfar North GA. The first of two cold fronts will move in on the \nbackside of this shortwave during the day Thursday, with the main \nimpact being increasingly breezy northwest winds. Temperatures \nThursday morning will start out in the 50s, and likely only climb \ninto the 60s before starting to fall again behind the front. Lows \nThursday night will likely fall to the upper 30s to low 40s for most.\n\nOn Friday, another shortwave rotating around the Eastern US trough \nwill move across the TN Valley, supporting slight precip chances (10-\n20%) in far North GA. While the probability is very low (<5%), \ntemperatures could be cool enough for this to fall as frozen precip \nin the highest elevations of the North GA mountains. A secondary \ncold front will move through behind the shortwave Friday, ushering \nin the coolest airmass of the season thus far for the weekend. \nModels remain consistent on low temperatures Saturday and Sunday \nmornings to be at or below freezing for most of the area, with 20s \nin the North GA mountains. While these temperatures would be 15 to \n20 degrees cooler than normal for this time of year, this would be \nright around normal for a first freeze (mid-November on average). \nHigh temperatures will likely struggle to climb out of the 50s for \nmost of the area, and 40s in the North GA mountains. Skies will be \nmostly clear, so the afternoon sun will help ease the refreshing \nchill in the air.",
